2494-### Pattern C: Durable Directive Plane
2495-
2496-- `spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` records a durable operator directive with acknowledgement state.
2497-- `spawn directives RUN-...` replays the directive history for that run.
2498-- `cancel` is applied immediately for queued runs. For a running prompt-mode run it terminates the background runner/process group; for a manager-hosted owner it also resolves `owner=run/RUN-...` and stops that provider through the Session Manager stop path before terminalizing the run. The stopped session detail names the operator cancel directive. A session already stopped by the deadline fence is accepted without a second stop; a manager stop failure does not hide the operator cancellation and is recorded on the run as `orphaned_child_session` with the failure detail.
2499-- A cancelled run routes its element (status rewound to the actionable queue, `cancelled by operator; operator action required` note) only when it OWNS the element — it holds the runner slot or the Story lease. Cancelling a queued duplicate behind a live owner cancels the run and leaves the element status, assignee and notes to the owner; the acknowledgement names that owner (`Element left to live owner RUN-...`). Ownership is identity, not queue order: a queued run never owns, and cancelling the running owner while a duplicate waits routes per the owner contract and leaves the duplicate queued, unpromoted. An unreadable ownership record — the Story lease or the run registry — is not an absent one, and a lease naming a run with no run record is neither: the element is left untouched and the acknowledgement says why. A lease still held by a terminal run owns nothing; the remaining sole run routes as before.
2500:- `pause_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, and `nudge` are cooperative signals. Current runtimes expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`; the child may poll `task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"` at safe checkpoints.
2501:- `restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` create successor `RUN-...` records instead of rewriting existing run history. Running reroute/pause requests are rejected when the runtime cannot checkpoint safely in place.
2502-- Both directive commands print the current active goal header before directive details. New records persist the immutable goal ID/revision under which they were issued; legacy records print `issued_under_goal: unknown`.
